  "name": "Transfer loot into a container automatically",
  "slug": "transfer-loot-into-a-container-automatically",
  "teaser": "Looting an item will now place it in a matching container, such as a dogtag case or a document case",
  "thumbnail": "https://forge-static.sp-tarkov.com/mods/1633.png",
  "downloads": 5175,
  "favourites_count": 0,
  "description": "<h3>Description</h3>\n<p>Using ctrl+click on a loot item will move it into a matching container marked with a @loot tag. For instance, if you mark your Documents case with a @loot tag and put it in your secure container, all keys you loot using ctrl+click will be placed in that documents case automatically. Works only in raid - will not work with your hideout stash!</p>\n<h3>Installation</h3>\n<p>Copy CactusPie.ContainerQuickLoot.dll to BepInEx\\plugins directory</p>\n<h3>Settings (F12 menu)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Enable for Ctrl+click - whether or not the items should be automatically transferred to containers when moving them using ctrl+click in the loot menu</li>\n<li>Enable for loose loot - whether or not loose loot you pick up should be automatically palced in a container. This works for all the loot you just find lying around on the ground, shelves, etc.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Usage</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Put a container in your inventory. For instance a Documents case:</li>\n<li>Right click the container and select the “tag” option</li>\n<li>Set a @loot tag for the item.</li>\n<li>Ctrl+clicking a compatible loot item (such as a key) in raid or picking up loose loot will now put it in the documents case</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Removing the @loot tag will stop the items from being automatically put in it</p>\n<p>You can also add numbered suffixes to @loot tags in order to give a certain container a priority. For instance a container with @loot2 tag will take higher priority than a container with @loot3 tag. Not specifying any suffix assumes a “0” suffix/priority. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>@loot will take higher priority than @loot1</li>\n<li>@loot10 will take higher priorty than @loot20</li>\n<li>In case of two containers with the same @loot tag, the game will automatically assign a higher priority to one</li>\n</ul>\n",
